      "output": {
        "decision": "answer",
        "reason": "The evidence contains hypotheses/explanations relevant to why Twitter better predicts the language inventory: the Discussion section notes Twitter has fewer missing languages and more false positives (suggesting it captures more diverse language use), and explains that Twitter matches populations better when weighted by GDP (representing wealthier/urban populations), while noting English and Spanish are very common on Twitter so countries using these languages are well represented. The paper also explicitly states more research is needed to explain sources of false positives. It does not give a single complete causal theory, but it does offer hypotheses/explanations for the Twitter advantage."
      },
      "elapsed_ms": 3360.2137999841943
    },
